# CLAUDE.md

Project rules for every Claude Code session in this repository, including the AI pipeline's agents.
Copy this file to `CLAUDE.md`, then replace the examples with your project's real rules. Keep it short: the
agents read it on every run.

## Project

- What this repository is, in two sentences.
- Main languages and frameworks.

## Commands

- Build and test: `./pipeline/scripts/run-checks.sh` (the same gate the pipeline uses).

## Architecture rules

- Example: controllers only map requests to commands; business logic lives in the application layer.
- Example: all database access goes through repositories.

## Conventions

- Example: follow the patterns of the surrounding code; match its naming and comment density.
- Example: every behaviour change comes with a test.

## Test layers

Every plan lists the tests a change needs in each layer it touches (the build writes them, the review checks each
one). Replace the example rows with your project's layers, without the "Example:", and delete the rows you don't
have (or the whole section: then the pipeline asks for unit tests of the changed behaviour). Rows still marked
"Example:" are ignored. List only layers `./pipeline/scripts/run-checks.sh` runs. Test IDs are the layer's ID (1–3
capital letters) and a number: U1, D1, …

| Layer | ID | What it proves | Where | Required when the change touches |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Example: Unit | U | Each function's or class's outcomes, including errors | `test/unit/` | any logic |
| Example: Database | D | Each query or stored function's results and constraints, against a throwaway database with every migration | `test/db/` | a migration, query or stored function |
| Example: API | A | The real API over HTTP: status codes and response bodies | `test/api/` | an endpoint, request or response |
| Example: UI | S | A real screen or component with its state: loading, error, empty, actions | `test/ui/` | a screen or component |
| Example: Journey | J | A whole user flow across screens, against a fake of the real API | `test/journeys/` | a user flow |

Optional: rules every user action meets (each gets a test where it applies; rules still marked "Example:" are
ignored):

1. Example: a write ignores repeat taps while it is in flight.
2. Example: a failed write keeps the user's input and says what failed.
3. Example: loading, error (with a retry) and empty states are shown honestly.

## Commit messages

[Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org): `type(scope): summary`, for example
`feat(api): add optional due dates to tasks`.

- Types: `feat` (new capability), `fix` (bug fix), `refactor` (no behaviour change), `perf`, `test` (tests only),
  `docs` (docs only), `ci` (CI workflows), `chore` (maintenance, tooling).
- Scope: the area changed, lowercase (for example `api`, `android`, `db`, `infra`).
- Summary: imperative, lowercase start, no trailing period, at most 72 characters.
- Breaking change: `!` after the scope (`feat(api)!: ...`) and a `BREAKING CHANGE: ...` footer.
- Footers: `Closes #123` or `Refs: #123`.

## Never

- Commit secrets, tokens or credentials.
- Change database migrations that have already run; add a new one.
- Edit `.github/`, `.claude/` or `pipeline/` from the pipeline.
